**Persona Key Findings:

* Mark needs a clear explanation of what the two-week Movement to Work programme involves before deciding whether to take part
* The transition from education into employment can feel unclear without structured guidance
* Limited work experience can make entry-level job requirements feel difficult to meet
* Mark needs help recognising the transferable skills he already has
* Transferable skills should be connected to real workplace examples so they are easier to understand and communicate to employers
* CV, application and interview support should be easy to locate
* Interview preparation is important for improving confidence before applying for roles
* Mark needs clear information about what employers expect from entry-level candidates
* Long or text-heavy content may be difficult to scan and could lead to disengagement
* Complex navigation may make employment support harder to access
* Content should be broken into short, clearly labelled sections with obvious next actions
* Mobile should be prioritised because Mark primarily accesses information through his phone
* Lengthy or complicated forms may create additional friction
* Local employment opportunities should be clearly presented so users can understand what is available nearby
* Travel information should be considered alongside local opportunities
* Mark needs to understand what support is available both during and after the programme
* Clear programme benefits should be shown, including experience, confidence, employability skills and CV development
* Interactive and visual content may improve engagement compared with large amounts of written information
* Guidance around appropriate AI use could help with CVs, applications and interview preparation
* The overall experience should provide a clear journey from learning about the programme through to becoming more work-ready and exploring employment opportunities
